xnu (12377.121.6+pie8) blueberry-pie-os; urgency=medium

  * A third kernel, rk3566, for the Rockchip RK3566 of the Anbernic
    RG353V: 16K pages, built for the Cortex-A55, with drivers for its
    GICv3, its DesignWare UART and watchdog and its SD card slots. pieboot
    picks it from the bundle by the board's compatible.
  * The Raspberry Pi's SDHCI driver and the RK3566's DesignWare MMC driver
    share the SD card protocol.
  * The kernel's console also goes to a RAM console that pieboot names,
    for the next system to read after a reset.
  * The watchdog driver also runs a DesignWare watchdog.
  * The xHCI driver leaves the RK3566's DWC3 USB controllers alone.
  * Making the first kext's text executable flushes the whole TLB. On the
    RK3566 its constructors took an instruction fetch fault on about one
    boot in eight.
  * kern.version names each kernel's build again, such as
    DEVELOPMENT_ARM64_BCM2712, so /etc/rc finds the SoC and loads the
    kexts.early lines tagged for it.
  * The early kexts for the Raspberry Pi's devices load on its kernels
    only. PIEBoard.kext and PIECPUFreq.kext match the Pi's mailbox, so
    kextd does not load them on the RK3566 either.
  * Kexts can drive USB devices through <pie/usb.h>. A bulk endpoint can
    keep its transfers queued, as a network adapter needs.
  * PIEUSBEthernet.kext drives USB Ethernet adapters with a Realtek
    RTL8152, RTL8153 or RTL8153B.
    It is not an early kext: kextd loads it when an adapter appears.
  * The xHCI driver refuses a USB device whose device or configuration
    descriptor comes back short or with a bad header, and uses only the
    whole descriptors of its configuration.
  * The xHCI driver aligns its DMA buffers by bus address. A 64 KiB bulk
    transfer could cross a 64 KiB boundary, and a USB storage device could
    stop working after about 15 attaches.
  * The xHCI driver runs a hub with multiple TTs in its single-TT default
    setting, and says so to the controller.
  * Device-tree properties with names of 32 to 255 characters, which
    pieboot passes encoded, reach drivers under their full names.
  * A root daemon checks in as the IOKit daemon with a DaemonReady
    request. The kernel then releases the registry root, starts the
    matching it held back and sends its kext load requests to kextd, so
    kexts load when a device matches them. After a soft reboot the check-in
    ends the reset that user space's restart began.
  * Only the kexts that kextd loads, and the libraries its loads bring in,
    even loads that fail, are unloaded when idle. The early kexts and kexts
    loaded by hand stay loaded.
  * getvfsbyname and lsvfs no longer spin for a file system that is not
    registered. vfs.generic.maxtypenum was 1836016492, from mockfs's type
    number, and now leaves it out. A file system that registers later
    never lowers it.
  * /usr/lib/blueberry-pie-os/kernels names each kernel's SoC and UUID, so
    kextd knows the SoCs and whether the running kernel is installed.
  * The preinst writes the boot session to
    /var/db/blueberry-pie-os-kernel.unpacking, and it is removed when the
    package is configured or removed. While it names the running boot,
    kextd and kextload load no kext from /System/Library/Extensions that
    is not loaded yet, as those may be the next kernel's. One from an
    earlier boot means the package was left unfinished: the kexts then
    load, with a warning, so that the boot partition can still be
    written. An aborted install or upgrade removes only a marker its own
    preinst made.
  * The postinst writes the boot session to
    /var/db/blueberry-pie-os-kernel.configured, so kextd and kextload
    wait for the reboot when the kernel changed. The postinst, and an
    aborted install or upgrade, have kextd scan again.
  * Root may add and remove IOKit personalities in the IOCatalogue.
  * The kernel depends on kext-tools 2, which has kextd, and on
    base-files 6, whose rc starts it.
  * An EHCI driver runs the RK3566's USB 2 host port behind the RG353V's
    host USB-C socket, for high-speed devices and high-speed hubs.
  * A new I2C driver reaches the RK817 PMIC, whose BOOST feeds that
    socket's 5 V. BOOST cannot be used with the charger, so the driver
    checks the charger's USB power every 50 ms. BOOST is turned off when
    that power comes, or once it has not read for 50 ms. If the charger's
    status stops reading as that power comes, or as the driver starts
    with it, BOOST can run with it for at most 240 ms, not counting time
    the CPU spends on other work. That holds while BOOST's own register,
    POWER_EN3, can be read and written. In simulation the longest was
    237.44 ms. When an access to POWER_EN3 fails, the OFF is tried again
    and logged, and BOOST runs with that power until the bus answers.
    BOOST is turned on while a check reads no USB power, from the boot
    volume on or a minute after start. It is turned off when the system
    halts or restarts. hw.battery.boost shows its state.
  * The RK817's charger and gas gauge show as a battery, RK817Battery,
    and as sysctls under hw.battery. On the RG353V the charger's USB
    input limit is set to 1.5 A.
  * The RK3566's SD card driver sets the card's IO supply and IO domain
    itself, and never lowers the supply toward 1.8 V. A card in 1.8 V
    signaling is kept at a supply the boot left at 1.8 V, without CMD11.
    Any other card or supply goes to 3.3 V. A card in 1.8 V signaling at
    another supply is left unused until the board is switched off and on.
    The RK817 driver sets the supply while BOOST is off, or while BOOST is
    on without USB power, in short slots that keep BOOST's bound. An
    orderly reboot of the RG353V's stock Linux leaves the second slot's IO
    supply at 0.6 V, where no card answered.
  * The RK3566's SD card driver waits to deadlines on the clock. Its waits
    counted IODelay's steps, which sleep on this kernel, so a wait could
    last far longer than it counted. A data transfer fails once its FIFO
    has not moved for 2 s. A card has 10 s to finish a write, and 10 s
    to leave its programming state after a failed transfer.
  * The RK3566's SD card driver logs its errors with the controller's
    status and their times, a FIFO reset that fails, and the steps of a
    recovery that does not end at its first try. It logs the controller's
    registers every 5 s while a transfer is out. The RK817 driver logs
    its LDOs' voltages at start.
  * Kexts can drive SDIO cards through <pie/sdio.h>. The kernel powers up
    and enumerates a card behind a host driver, publishes a nub for each
    function, and does CMD52 and CMD53 transfers and interrupts for the
    function drivers. A function driver can have the card powered up and
    set up again.
  * On the Raspberry Pi 4 and 500, the WiFi chip's slot runs as an SDIO
    host, so a kext can drive its CYW43455. The kernel powers the chip
    through WL_ON. The boot-arg pie_sdio=0 leaves the slot to the SD card
    driver.

 -- Maxwell <maxwell@blueberrypieos.com>  Wed, 18 Nov 2026 15:00:00 +0000

xnu (12377.121.6+pie6) blueberry-pie-os; urgency=medium

  * A pool on file vdevs opens: a file's attributes now include its type.
  * File vdevs are closed on unmount only while the system shuts down. A
    dataset unmount closed them under I/O, which gave read errors.
  * A zvol's I/O runs on the zvol taskqs and reports its errors. A pool on
    a zvol of another pool hung under writes and at import, and a failed
    zvol read or write reported success.
  * A remount does not make a dataset writable while its readonly property
    is on, so a read-only root stays read-only.
  * A remount that names another dataset than the one mounted is refused.
    It renamed the mount's source to that dataset.
  * A remount holds the file system's teardown lock as a reader, so a
    rollback or receive cannot replace the dataset under it.
  * A rollback or receive no longer purges the name cache while it holds
    the file system's teardown lock, where a vnode reclaim could deadlock.
  * A last zvol close that waited for the suspend lock keeps it, and an
    open or close that retries does not release a suspend lock it gave up.
  * blueberry-pie-os-kernel-dev installs the headers of /usr/local/include
    in /usr/include. A ZFS root keeps /usr/local on its data sets, where
    dpkg installs nothing.
  * The kernel maps the watchdog's registers at every boot. Its first try
    could come before their addresses were known. It then left running
    the watchdog that pieboot arms for a fallback boot, which reset the
    board about 16 s into the boot.
  * When the kernel cannot read the firmware's revision, it still sets or
    disarms a watchdog that pieboot armed. So does the watchdog driver. A
    failed map of the watchdog's registers is tried again.
  * A firmware call made before the mailbox is ready no longer leaks its
    message buffer.
  * The root pool imports from a card that held a larger pool before it
    was written again. The boot import read the whole disk too, found the
    old pool's labels at the card's end and took them for the new pool's.
  * On the Pi 4, whose kernel has smaller pages than user space, data a
    process passed to the kernel is freed again. Its last page stayed
    mapped in the kernel and kept the whole buffer, so every kext load
    leaked it, about 4 MB at each soft reboot.

 -- Maxwell <maxwell@blueberrypieos.com>  Mon, 16 Nov 2026 18:28:11 +0000

xnu (12377.121.6+pie4) blueberry-pie-os; urgency=medium

  * ZFS is built into the kernel: OpenZFS 2.4.1, which mounts a pool's
    boot dataset as the root with zfs_boot=POOL in the boot-args.
  * The ARC's maximum is a quarter of the memory and its minimum 64 MiB
    by default; zfs_arc_max= and zfs_arc_min= in the boot-args set other
    sizes, within the limits ZFS accepts.
  * Process 1 may switch the root with pivot_root(2) and unmount the old
    root, for a restart of user space on another root.
  * The kernel package provides pie-zfs-abi (= 2.4), the ZFS interface the
    zfs tools need.
  * A pool's boot dataset mounted as the root names the dataset as the
    mount's source.
  * The postinst updates the boot partition only when rpi-firmware's
    blueberry-pie-os-update-boot is installed.
  * ZFS encryption uses the PMULL instructions for GCM where the CPU has
    them, as the Pi 5's does.

